[
    {
        "content": "<p>Lean accepts this proof without complaint:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">first</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">xyzzy</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>However, the theorem <code>xyzzy</code> does not exist.  I would expect Lean to produce an error message about that.  This confused me when I was debugging recently - it took me a while to realize that I had simply mistyped the name of a theorem in a tactic block.</p>\n<p>The problem is not unique to <code>first</code> or <code>apply</code>.  For example, Lean also accepts this:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">try</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">xyzzy</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The problem seems to be that any tactic combinator (such as <code>first</code> or <code>try</code>) will treat an unknown identifier error in its body just the same as any other failure.</p>\n<p>I'm tempted to report this as a bug - does that seem reasonable?  Or is this behavior just considered to be the way it's expected to work?</p>",
        "id": 617009016,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1786986684
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Well, this behavior is intentional; if anything inside <code>first</code> fails (except for the last branch), you get no errors.</p>",
        "id": 617011247,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1786987556
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>And <code>try tac</code> is literally just <code>first | tac | skip</code></p>",
        "id": 617011346,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1786987593
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sure.  Still, it seems a bit limiting to me that there is only one notion of failure here.  To me, an unknown identifier is a different type of problem than a tactic that searched for a proof and didn't find one.  If I have an unknown identifier anywhere in my program, I'd like to know that even before it starts to run.</p>",
        "id": 617011497,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1786987657
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Okay, what do you think about this then:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">repeat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">first</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>This would be a tactic that can solve goals of the shape <code>... -&gt; p -&gt; p</code>. In this case we wouldn't want to get an unknown identifier error for <code>h</code>, would we?<br>\nSure, you could write this differently, but the point is that there are legitimate reasons why unknown identifier errors should be silenced.</p>",
        "id": 617012311,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1786987894
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Also, I have a followup question.  Suppose that I've written my own tactic combinator using a macro, e.g.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">macro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"my_apply\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"n\">ident</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">tactic</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">tactic</span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">first</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">my_apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">xyzzy</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>my_apply</code> may succeed even when given an invalid tactic name:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">my_apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">xyzzy</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I'd like to modify <code>my_apply</code> so that it will fail if given an invalid tactic name.  To do that, I think it will no longer be able to be a macro, because the <code>MacroM</code> monad is too limited.  So probably I'll need something like this:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">elab</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"my_apply\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"n\">term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">tactic</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">do</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">elabTerm</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">none</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- what now?</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Presumably if the given name is invalid then <code>elabTerm</code> will fail.  If it succeeds, I'd like to construct the syntax <code>`(tactic| first | apply $e | simp)</code>, then run that tactic.  But that won't work, because <code>e</code> is an <code>Expr</code>, not a piece of syntax.  Perhaps I need to fall back on simply calling the code that the <code>first</code>, <code>apply</code> and <code>simp</code> tactics would run, but if anyone has any better idea, I'd be happy to hear it.</p>",
        "id": 617013012,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1786988089
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You could also use let bindings:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">macro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"my_apply\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"n\">ident</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">tactic</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">tactic</span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">first</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">my_apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">xyzzy</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 617013517,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1786988217
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Wow - I didn't realize that I could put a <code>let</code> into a tactic block, and use the resulting variable in that way.  This is clever, and solves my problem nicely, without my needing to write an elaborator.  Thanks very much!</p>",
        "id": 617015376,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1786988693
    }
]